data artifact_store_fs_transport_note: String = "P2 host-transport half (witness-realization plan): the filesystem realization of std.artifact_store's persistent tier. The keyed path ENCODES identity - store_root/<content-hash>.artifact - so presence-at-path is a ContentKeyed hit for exactly that identity, which is NOT the #6352 existence-keying (that red keyed on OUTPUT presence while the INPUTS went unhashed; here the path IS the input hash). Layering: this module is one transport handler bound to the pure spec (std.artifact_store, the single authority); a second handler (e.g. remote CAS) binds the same shape. Grant refusals surface as typed ArtifactFs*Refused variants (verb, target, frame) — never widened to miss/false/[] (DESIGN 5). SCOPE (corrected 2026-07-29, operator-flagged on PR 7385): this sentence previously claimed the cited Filesystem service exposed no Delete/List and that SpacePacked enforcement was therefore unrealizable. That was FALSE and had been for a long time - extdeps.filesystem.filesystem_io declares both operations, artifact_fs_delete and artifact_fs_list below realize them, and artifact_store_fs_witness_test exercises the put-list-delete-list round trip green. It is a parallel-ledger note that sat above the functions refuting it and was believed over them (it was then propagated into a second module before being caught). The REAL gap it obscured was narrower: std.artifact_store's evict_until_within_budget computed a StorePutReceipt carrying evicted keys, and nothing passed those keys to artifact_fs_delete - the eviction DECISION was computed and discarded at the host boundary, so the disk tier grew unbounded because no caller invoked the verb, not because the verb was missing. apply_store_evictions below closes that: it applies a receipt's evictions through this transport with every per-key outcome typed and counted, so a grant refusal is a recorded EvictionRefused rather than a silent skip."
=======
data artifact_store_fs_transport_note: String = "P2 host-transport half (witness-realization plan): the filesystem realization of std.artifact_store's persistent tier. The keyed path ENCODES identity - store_root/<content-hash>.artifact - so presence-at-path is a ContentKeyed hit for exactly that identity, which is NOT the #6352 existence-keying (that red keyed on OUTPUT presence while the INPUTS went unhashed; here the path IS the input hash). Layering: this module is one transport handler bound to the pure spec (std.artifact_store, the single authority); a second handler (e.g. remote CAS) binds the same shape. Grant refusals surface as typed ArtifactFs*Refused variants (verb, target, frame) — never widened to miss/false/[] (DESIGN 5). SCOPE (corrected 2026-07-29, operator-flagged on PR 7385): this sentence previously claimed the cited Filesystem service exposed no Delete/List and that SpacePacked enforcement was therefore unrealizable. That was FALSE and had been for a long time - extdeps.filesystem.filesystem_io declares both operations, artifact_fs_delete and artifact_fs_list below realize them, and artifact_store_fs_witness_test exercises the put-list-delete-list round trip green. It is a parallel-ledger note that sat above the functions refuting it and was believed over them (it was then propagated into a second module before being caught). The REAL gap it obscured was narrower: std.artifact_store's evict_until_within_budget computed a StorePutReceipt carrying evicted keys, and nothing passed those keys to artifact_fs_delete - the eviction DECISION was computed and discarded at the host boundary, so the disk tier grew unbounded because no caller invoked the verb, not because the verb was missing. apply_store_evictions below closes that: it applies those evicted keys through this transport with every per-key outcome typed and counted, so a grant refusal is a recorded EvictionRefused rather than a silent skip."
